              "id": "glog-e0316492-4393-45e5-a780-e6928dc90f52",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in the C++ code arises from the use of the `memcpy` function, which can lead to buffer overflow if the destination buffer is not large enough to accommodate the data being copied. In this specific case, the code attempts to copy a `uint64_t` value into a buffer without ensuring that the buffer has sufficient space, which can result in memory corruption, crashes, or potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Buffer Size Validation**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer functions like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` that provide better safety guarantees.\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to ensure that the copy operation does not exceed the buffer limits.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in the code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the vulnerability, ensure that the buffer has enough space before performing the `memcpy` operation. Here is a revised version of the code with added checks:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\n// Assuming these are defined somewhere in the code\nstruct Axis {\n    uint64_t binarySize;\n};\n\nstruct FFTPlan {\n    std::vector<std::vector<Axis>> axes;\n};\n\nstruct Application {\n    FFTPlan* localFFTPlan;\n};\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* destination, size_t destSize, const void* source, size_t num) {\n    if (num <= destSize) {\n        std::memcpy(destination, source, num);\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Buffer overflow prevented: source size exceeds destination size.\" << std::endl;\n        // Handle error appropriately\n    }\n}\n\nvoid exampleFunction(Application* app, char* localApplicationStringCast, size_t bufferSize) {\n    size_t currentPos = 0;\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < app->localFFTPlan->axes.size(); ++i) {\n        for (size_t j = 0; j < app->localFFTPlan->axes[i].size(); ++j) {\n            if (currentPos + sizeof(uint64_t) <= bufferSize) {\n                safeMemcpy(localApplicationStringCast + currentPos, bufferSize - currentPos,\n                           &app->localFFTPlan->axes[i][j].binarySize, sizeof(uint64_t));\n                currentPos += sizeof(uint64_t);\n            } else {\n                std::cerr << \"Insufficient buffer size for memcpy operation.\" << std::endl;\n                // Handle error appropriately\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard C++ libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstdint>`: For fixed-width integer types like `uint64_t`.\n- `<cstring>`: For memory manipulation functions like `memcpy`.\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n- `<vector>`: For using the `std::vector` container.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-cf50fa73-7acc-4d17-be6c-ff363319834a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The specific issue is related to the potential for buffer overflow or memory corruption when copying data into a buffer without proper bounds checking. In the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is used to copy data into `localApplicationStringCast + currentPos` from `&app->localFFTPlan_inverse->R2Cdecomposition.binarySize` with a size of `sizeof(uint64_t)`. If `localApplicationStringCast` does not have enough space to accommodate the copied data, this can lead to undefined behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the buffer before performing the copy operation.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `memmove` or C++ standard library functions like `std::copy`, which provide better type safety and can help prevent buffer overflows.\n\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer has enough space before performing the `memcpy` operation. Here is a revised version of the code snippet with added bounds checking:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n\n// Assuming these are defined somewhere in your code\nstruct FFTPlan {\n    struct {\n        uint64_t binarySize;\n    } R2Cdecomposition;\n};\n\nstruct Application {\n    FFTPlan* localFFTPlan_inverse;\n};\n\n// Function to safely copy data\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* destination, size_t destSize, size_t currentPos, const uint64_t* source, size_t size) {\n    if (currentPos + size <= destSize) {\n        std::memcpy(destination + currentPos, source, size);\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Buffer overflow prevented!\" << std::endl;\n        // Handle error appropriately\n    }\n}\n\n// Example usage\nint main() {\n    const size_t bufferSize = 1024;\n    char localApplicationStringCast[bufferSize];\n    size_t currentPos = 0;\n    Application app;\n    FFTPlan fftPlan;\n    fftPlan.R2Cdecomposition.binarySize = 123456789;\n    app.localFFTPlan_inverse = &fftPlan;\n\n    safeMemcpy(localApplicationStringCast, bufferSize, currentPos, &app.localFFTPlan_inverse->R2Cdecomposition.binarySize, sizeof(uint64_t));\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard C++ libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstdint>`: For fixed-width integer types.\n- `<cstring>`: For the `memcpy` function.\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)"
              },
