              "id": "glog-3f708301-b282-44c9-b26b-3abcb654056c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ typically arises from improper use of the `memcpy` function, which can lead to buffer overflows if the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied. This can result in undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, and security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is used to copy data from `app->localFFTPlan_inverse->inverseBluesteinAxes[i][j].binary` to `localApplicationStringCast + currentPos`. If the destination buffer (`localApplicationStringCast`) is not adequately sized to accommodate the data being copied, a buffer overflow can occur.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Buffer Size**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer against the size of the data to be copied.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory areas are involved, or `std::vector` and `std::array` for automatic bounds checking.\n\n3. **Input Validation**: Validate all inputs to ensure they are within expected ranges and sizes before performing memory operations.\n\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. Here is a revised version of the code snippet with added checks:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // For memcpy\n#include <stdexcept> // For std::runtime_error\n\n// Assuming localApplicationStringCast is a char array and has a known size\nconst size_t localApplicationStringCastSize = /* size of the buffer */;\n\nif (currentPos + app->localFFTPlan_inverse->inverseBluesteinAxes[i][j].binarySize > localApplicationStringCastSize) {\n    throw std::runtime_error(\"Buffer overflow detected: destination buffer is too small.\");\n}\n\nmemcpy(localApplicationStringCast + currentPos, \n       app->localFFTPlan_inverse->inverseBluesteinAxes[i][j].binary, \n       app->localFFTPlan_inverse->inverseBluesteinAxes[i][j].binarySize);\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following library dependencies are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For the `memcpy` function.\n- `<stdexcept>`: For exception handling with `std::runtime_error`.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
