              "id": "glog-3cac8fca-cd4a-416a-809d-254e95ae8966",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C++, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used carefully. The specific issue is with the line:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(localApplicationStringCast + currentPos, &app->localFFTPlan_inverse->inverseBluesteinAxes[i][j].binarySize, sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\n\nThis line copies data from a source to a destination buffer without checking if the destination buffer has enough space to accommodate the data being copied. If `localApplicationStringCast` does not have enough space starting from `currentPos`, this can lead to a buffer overflow, potentially causing undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer has enough space to accommodate the data being copied. This can be done by maintaining a variable that tracks the size of the buffer and checking it before performing the copy operation.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are involved, and ensure that the size of the destination buffer is checked.\n\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regular code reviews can help catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer has enough space before performing the `memcpy` operation. Here is a revised version of the code with added bounds checking:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n\n// Assuming these are defined somewhere in your code\nconst size_t BUFFER_SIZE = 1024; // Example buffer size\nuint8_t localApplicationStringCast[BUFFER_SIZE];\nsize_t currentPos = 0;\n\n// Example structure\nstruct FFTPlan {\n    struct Axis {\n        uint64_t binarySize;\n    } inverseBluesteinAxes[10][10];\n};\n\nstruct Application {\n    FFTPlan* localFFTPlan_inverse;\n};\n\n// Function to safely copy data\nvoid safeMemcpy(Application* app, size_t i, size_t j) {\n    if (currentPos + sizeof(uint64_t) <= BUFFER_SIZE) {\n        memcpy(localApplicationStringCast + currentPos, &app->localFFTPlan_inverse->inverseBluesteinAxes[i][j].binarySize, sizeof(uint64_t));\n        currentPos += sizeof(uint64_t);\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Buffer overflow prevented!\" << std::endl;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstdint>`: For fixed-width integer types.\n- `<cstring>`: For memory handling functions like `memcpy`.\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
