              "id": "glog-3daca184-25e3-454d-858b-da8fee0288c9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question involves the use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The `memcpy` function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. The issue arises when the size of the source memory block exceeds the size of the destination memory block, leading to a buffer overflow. This can result in undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, and security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is used as follows:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(localApplicationStringCast + currentPos, app->localFFTPlan_inverse->axes[i][j].binary, app->localFFTPlan_inverse->axes[i][j].binarySize);\n```\n\nThe vulnerability occurs if `app->localFFTPlan_inverse->axes[i][j].binarySize` is larger than the available space in `localApplicationStringCast` starting from `currentPos`.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by performing explicit bounds checking before calling `memcpy`.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` which provide better safety guarantees and are part of the C++ Standard Library.\n\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly conduct code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer has enough space to accommodate the data being copied. Here is a revised version of the code with added bounds checking:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t availableSpace = /* calculate available space in localApplicationStringCast starting from currentPos */;\nsize_t dataSize = app->localFFTPlan_inverse->axes[i][j].binarySize;\n\nif (dataSize <= availableSpace) {\n    memcpy(localApplicationStringCast + currentPos, app->localFFTPlan_inverse->axes[i][j].binary, dataSize);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: insufficient space in destination buffer\n    // This could involve logging an error, throwing an exception, or other error handling mechanisms\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided relies on the following standard C++ libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for the `memcpy` function.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
