            {
              "id": "glog-2e2401eb-2b1e-42fc-acef-7ad07241ceb8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the improper use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The function call:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(&app->applicationStringSize, app->configuration.loadApplicationString, sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\n\nis potentially unsafe because it assumes that `app->configuration.loadApplicationString` points to a valid memory region of at least `sizeof(uint64_t)` bytes. If this assumption is incorrect, it can lead to buffer overflows, data corruption, or undefined behavior, which are common security vulnerabilities in C++ programs.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Ensure that the source buffer is large enough to accommodate the number of bytes being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Functions:** Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are possible.\n3. **Bounds Checking:** Implement bounds checking to ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools:** Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in the code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability, ensure that the source buffer is valid and large enough before performing the copy operation. Here is a revised version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for std::memcpy\n#include <cstdint> // Required for uint64_t\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::copy\n\n// Assuming app->configuration.loadApplicationString is a pointer to uint64_t\nif (app->configuration.loadApplicationString != nullptr) {\n    std::copy(\n        app->configuration.loadApplicationString,\n        app->configuration.loadApplicationString + 1,\n        &app->applicationStringSize\n    );\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For `std::memcpy` (if used).\n- `<cstdint>`: For fixed-width integer types like `uint64_t`.\n- `<algorithm>`: For `std::copy`.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
