            {
              "id": "glog-11f640c5-1e8f-470a-8018-d9c8efa7695e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (malloc)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `malloc` function is used incorrectly, leading to potential memory allocation issues. Specifically, the vulnerability occurs when `malloc` is called with an incorrect size calculation, such as `malloc(sizeof(size_t))`. This can lead to insufficient memory allocation, buffer overflows, or undefined behavior, as the allocated memory may not be sufficient for the intended use.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Correct Size Calculation**: Always ensure that the size passed to `malloc` is correctly calculated based on the number of elements and the size of each element.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using C++ memory management features like `new` or smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) which handle memory allocation and deallocation more safely.\n3. **Check Return Values**: Always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that memory allocation was successful before using the allocated memory.\n4. **Initialize Allocated Memory**: Use functions like `calloc` which initialize the allocated memory to zero, reducing the risk of using uninitialized memory.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a corrected version of the code that properly allocates memory for a `size_t` variable:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>  // Required for malloc and free\n#include <iostream> // Required for std::cout\n\nint main() {\n    // Correct size calculation for a single size_t element\n    size_t* ptr = static_cast<size_t*>(malloc(sizeof(size_t)));\n    \n    if (ptr == nullptr) {\n        std::cerr << \"Memory allocation failed\" << std::endl;\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Use the allocated memory\n    *ptr = 42;\n    std::cout << \"Value: \" << *ptr << std::endl;\n\n    // Free the allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following standard libraries to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: Provides the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n- `<iostream>`: Provides input and output stream objects like `std::cout` and `std::cerr`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-122: Heap-based Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/122.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
