              "id": "glog-3e8a4dee-aeef-414c-b6b8-02c894e08cd4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Detected Issue with C Function (malloc)\" in C++ programming arises when the `malloc` function is used improperly, potentially leading to memory allocation issues such as buffer overflows, memory leaks, or undefined behavior. In the provided code snippet, the expression `malloc(app->configuration.autoCustomBluesteinPaddingPattern * sizeof(uint64_t))` is used to allocate memory. If `app->configuration.autoCustomBluesteinPaddingPattern` is not properly validated, it could lead to an integer overflow or excessive memory allocation, which can be exploited by attackers to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all inputs used in memory allocation are validated to prevent integer overflows and excessive memory allocation.\n2. **Check Return Values**: Always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that memory allocation was successful.\n3. **Use Safe Functions**: Consider using safer alternatives or wrappers that include bounds checking.\n4. **Limit Memory Usage**: Implement limits on memory allocation to prevent excessive resource consumption.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <iostream>\n\n// Assuming app and configuration are defined elsewhere\nstruct Configuration {\n    size_t autoCustomBluesteinPaddingPattern;\n};\n\nstruct App {\n    Configuration configuration;\n};\n\nApp* app = new App();\n\nvoid* safeMalloc(size_t count, size_t size) {\n    if (count == 0 || size == 0 || count > SIZE_MAX / size) {\n        std::cerr << \"Invalid memory allocation request\" << std::endl;\n        return nullptr;\n    }\n    void* ptr = malloc(count * size);\n    if (!ptr) {\n        std::cerr << \"Memory allocation failed\" << std::endl;\n    }\n    return ptr;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    app->configuration.autoCustomBluesteinPaddingPattern = 10; // Example value\n    uint64_t* buffer = static_cast<uint64_t*>(safeMalloc(app->configuration.autoCustomBluesteinPaddingPattern, sizeof(uint64_t)));\n    if (buffer) {\n        // Use the buffer\n        free(buffer);\n    }\n    delete app;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstdint>`: For fixed-width integer types.\n- `<cstdlib>`: For memory allocation functions like `malloc` and `free`.\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html)"
