                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The specific line of code:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(&codeSize, localStrPointer, sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\n\nThis line attempts to copy data from `localStrPointer` into `codeSize`, assuming that `localStrPointer` points to a valid memory location containing at least `sizeof(uint64_t)` bytes. If `localStrPointer` does not point to a valid memory location or if it points to a location with insufficient data, this can lead to undefined behavior, including buffer overflows, data corruption, or crashes.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Validate Input:** Ensure that `localStrPointer` is not null and points to a valid memory location with at least `sizeof(uint64_t)` bytes available.\n2. **Use Safer Functions:** Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory areas are a concern.\n3. **Bounds Checking:** Implement bounds checking to ensure that the source and destination buffers are of appropriate sizes.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools:** Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other memory issues.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo mitigate the vulnerability, you can add checks to ensure that `localStrPointer` is valid and use safer functions:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>  // For std::memcpy\n#include <cstdint>  // For uint64_t\n#include <stdexcept> // For std::runtime_error\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(uint64_t* dest, const char* src, size_t srcSize) {\n    if (src == nullptr) {\n        throw std::runtime_error(\"Source pointer is null\");\n    }\n    if (srcSize < sizeof(uint64_t)) {\n        throw std::runtime_error(\"Source size is too small\");\n    }\n    std::memcpy(dest, src, sizeof(uint64_t));\n}\n\n// Usage\ntry {\n    safeMemcpy(&codeSize, localStrPointer, localStrSize);\n} catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For `std::memcpy`.\n- `<cstdint>`: For `uint64_t`.\n- `<stdexcept>`: For `std::runtime_error`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
