            {
              "id": "glog-5df13557-bc0d-4fb3-b441-420ee45c66a3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question arises from the use of the `memcpy` function in C++. The function call:\n\n```cpp\nmemcpy(code, localStrPointer + sizeof(uint64_t), codeSize)\n```\n\nis potentially unsafe because it does not ensure that the source and destination buffers are appropriately sized, which can lead to buffer overflows. This can result in undefined behavior, including data corruption, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as arbitrary code execution.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Bounds Checking**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination buffers before performing the copy operation.\n\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `std::copy` or `std::memmove` if overlapping memory regions are possible, or `std::vector` for automatic bounds checking.\n\n3. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n4. **Code Reviews**: Regularly perform code reviews to catch potential vulnerabilities early in the development process.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability, ensure that the destination buffer `code` is large enough to accommodate `codeSize` bytes. Here is a revised version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <cstdint> // Required for uint64_t\n\nvoid safeMemcpy(char* code, const char* localStrPointer, size_t codeSize, size_t codeBufferSize) {\n    if (codeSize <= codeBufferSize) {\n        memcpy(code, localStrPointer + sizeof(uint64_t), codeSize);\n    } else {\n        // Handle error: code buffer is not large enough\n        // This could be logging an error, throwing an exception, etc.\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the code example properly, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstring>`: For `memcpy`.\n- `<cstdint>`: For `uint64_t`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
